          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the late 1920s,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugene+Wigner"} Eugene Wigner
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Weyl"} Hermann Weyl
            highlighted the role that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+theory"} group theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            play in the analysis of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atomic+spectrum"} atomic spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            had more or less explicitly been observed before, Wigner and Weyl stood out as making the mathematical formalism fully explicit. This attitude was not well received by some of their colleagues, who felt that this formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            had no place in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erwin+Schr%C3%B6dinger"} Erwin Schrödinger
            is said (
            %a{:href => "#Wigner81"}> Wigner 1981
            ) to have spoken of the
            %em
              %strong Gruppenpest
            (German for “plague of group theory”) which ought to be abandoned.
          %p
            In his
            %a{:href => "#JS75"} autobiography
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Slater"} John Slater
          %blockquote
            %p It was at this point that Wigner, Hund, Heitler, and Weyl entered the picture with their “Gruppenpest”: the pest of the group theory… The authors of the “Gruppenpest” wrote papers which were incomprehensible to those like me who had not studied group theory, in which they applied these theoretical results to the study of the many electron problem. The practical consequences appeared to be negligible, but everyone felt that to be in the mainstream one had to learn about it. Yet there were no good texts from which one could learn group theory. It was a frustrating experience, worthy of the name of a pest.
            %p I had what I can only describe as a feeling of outrage at the turn which the subject had taken…
            %p As soon as this &amp;lbrack;Slater’s&amp;rbrack; paper became known, it was obvious that a great many other physicists were as disgusted as I had been with the group-theoretical approach to the problem. As I heard later, there were remarks made such as “Slater has slain the ‘Gruppenpest’”. I believe that no other piece of work I have done was so universally popular.
          %p
            Eventually this resistance vanished and turned into its opposite in theoretical fundamental physics: in the classification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+representations+of+the+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} unitary representations of the Poincaré group
            introduced by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Weyl"} Hermann Weyl
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            in terms of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+groups"} gauge groups
          %p
            Today almost the first thing that one wants to know about any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} physical theory
            is its
            = succeed "-group" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+symmetry"} global symmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            and the their relevant
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
          %h2#references References
          %p Historical texts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Weyl"}> Hermann Weyl
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantenmechanik und Gruppentheorie
                %strong 46
                (1927) 1–46 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02055756"}> doi:10.1007/BF02055756
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Weyl"}> Hermann Weyl
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gruppentheorie und Quantenmechanik
                %em The Theory of Groups and Quantum Mechanics
                Dover (1950) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://store.doverpublications.com/0486602699.html"}> ISBN:0486602699
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/ost-chemistry-quantumtheoryofa029235mbp/page/n15/mode/2up"} ark:/13960/t1kh1w36w
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugene+P.+Wigner"}> Eugene P. Wigner
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gruppentheorie und ihre Anwendung auf die Quantenmechanik der Atomspektren
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-663-02555-9"} doi:10.1007/978-3-663-02555-9
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://digbib.ubka.uni-karlsruhe.de/volltexte/wasbleibt/33355459/33355459.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugene+P.+Wigner"}> Eugene P. Wigner
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Group theory: And its application to the quantum mechanics of atomic spectra
              %p
                Press (1959) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.elsevier.com/books/group-theory/wigner/978-0-12-750550-3"}> doi:978-0-12-750550-3
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Transcript of an interview with Wigner where he mentions Schrödinger’s remark on the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em Gruppenpest
          %ul
            %li#Wigner81
              Interview with
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugene+Wigner"} Eugene Wigner
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.aip.org/history-programs/niels-bohr-library/oral-histories/4965"} Niels Bohr Library and Archives
          %p History and review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+G.+Wybourne"}> Brian G. Wybourne
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The “The Gruppen Pest” yesterday, today, and tomorrow
                %strong 7
                (1973) 35-43 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1002/qua.560070706"}> doi:10.1002/qua.560070706
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Erhard Scholz,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introducing groups into quantum theory (1926-1930)
                %strong 33
                4 (2006) 440-490 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0409571"}> arXiv:math/0409571
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hm.2005.11.007"} doi:10.1016/j.hm.2005.11.007
            %li
              %p
                Erhard Scholz,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Weyl entering the ‘new’ quantum mechanics discourse
                = succeed "," do
                  %em HQ-1: Conference on the History of Quantum Physics
                %strong 350
                II (2008) 253–271. (Berlin July 2–6, 2007) &amp;lbrack;
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Scholz-WeylEntering.pdf", :title => "pdfl"}> pdfl
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Arianna Borrelli, Bretislav Friedrich,
                %em Eugene Wigner and the bliss of the “Gruppenpest”
                (2011) &amp;lbrack;
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Borrelli-Friedrich_BlissOfGruppenpest.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
